<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Stand on the Ugra River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1480">1480</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Stand on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugra-river">Ugra River</a> was a stand-off in 1480 between the forces of Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and the army of the Great Horde on opposite banks of the Ugra River. The Horde's army withdrew without a decisive battle. The episode is traditionally taken to mark the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> overlordship over Muscovy, a milestone in the foundation story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-stand-on-the-ugra-river.jpg" alt="Great Stand on the Ugra River" width="227" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facial_Chronicle_-_b.16,_p._462_-_Great_standing_on_the_Ugra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
